      "topic": {
        "text": "postfix increment operator behavior",
        "refs": ["Q0000"]
      },
      "explanation_type": null,
      "explanation_text": {
        "text": "When the increment operator is used as a postfix (e.g., X++), the current value of X is first copied to the target variable, and then X is incremented by 1. Thus after `Y = X++`, Y receives the original value of X and X becomes the original value plus one.",
        "refs": ["Q0000"]
      }
    },
    {
      "topic": {
        "text": "prefix increment operator behavior",
        "refs": ["Q0001"]
      },
      "explanation_type": null,
      "explanation_text": {
        "text": "When the increment operator is used as a prefix (e.g., ++X), X is first incremented by 1, and then the new value is copied to the target variable. Thus after `Y = ++X`, both X and Y contain the incremented value.",
        "refs": ["Q0001"]
      }
    },
    {
      "topic": {
        "text": "augmented (compound) assignment operators",
        "refs": ["Q0002"]
      },
      "explanation_type": null,
      "explanation_text": {
        "text": "Augmented assignment operators combine an arithmetic operation with assignment, such as `X += 2` which adds 2 to X and stores the result back in X. They provide a shorter syntax equivalent to `X = X + 2`. Similar operators exist for subtraction (`-=`), multiplication (`*=`), and division (`/=`).",
        "refs": ["Q0002"]
      }
    },
    {
      "topic": {
        "text": "operator precedence in arithmetic expressions",
        "refs": ["Q0003", "Q0004"]
      },
      "explanation_type": null,
      "explanation_text": {
        "text": "In arithmetic expressions, multiplication and division have higher precedence than addition and subtraction, so they are evaluated first. Parentheses can be used to override the default precedence and force a different evaluation order.",
        "refs": ["Q0003", "Q0004"]
      }
    }
  ],
  "examples": [
